1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,707
Spin our motor faster, and its back-EMF
grows with the speed.

2
00:00:04,747 --> 00:00:11,747
But from a 24 V bus, linear SVPWM makes at
most 13.9 V of phase amplitude.

3
00:00:11,787 --> 00:00:17,474
At full current, the motor needs all of it
at 5,030 rpm.

4
00:00:17,514 --> 00:00:20,745
Today, in the series finale: running past
that limit,

5
00:00:20,785 --> 00:00:25,419
finding the rotor angle without a sensor,
and where the power goes.

6
00:00:26,419 --> 00:00:28,592
First, Lesson 20's answers.

7
00:00:28,632 --> 00:00:32,246
One: on a surface-magnet motor, torque has
no i_d term,

8
00:00:32,286 --> 00:00:35,502
so at maximum current the best i_d is
zero.

9
00:00:35,542 --> 00:00:41,785
Two: with 20 A on q, the flux linkage is
6.58 mWb,

10
00:00:41,825 --> 00:00:48,342
and 13.9 V over that gives 2107 rad/s:
5,030 rpm.

11
00:00:48,382 --> 00:00:53,191
Three: above base speed, i_d must go
negative. That's field weakening.

12
00:00:54,191 --> 00:00:57,339
Draw both limits in the plane of i_d and
i_q.

13
00:00:57,379 --> 00:01:00,431
The current limit is a circle of 20 A.

14
00:01:00,471 --> 00:01:06,057
Neglect R, and the voltage is jωe times
the flux linkage ψ,

15
00:01:06,097 --> 00:01:09,509
so |ψ| must stay below V_max/ωe.

16
00:01:09,549 --> 00:01:13,514
That's a second circle, centred at i_d =
−λm/L:

17
00:01:13,554 --> 00:01:19,537
the characteristic current, 130 A, six and
a half times our limit.

18
00:01:19,577 --> 00:01:22,503
As the speed rises, it shrinks toward that
centre.

19
00:01:22,544 --> 00:01:26,010
The operating point must stay inside both
circles.

20
00:01:27,010 --> 00:01:31,960
At base speed, the voltage circle passes
through full torque: 20 A on q.

21
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,468
Go faster, and the current must swing left
along its circle:

22
00:01:35,508 --> 00:01:38,206
i_d goes negative, and the torque falls.

23
00:01:38,246 --> 00:01:44,964
At 5,500 rpm, i_d is −10.9 A and the
torque 0.65 N·m.

24
00:01:45,004 --> 00:01:52,631
At 6,014 rpm, all 20 A sit on −d, and the
torque is zero.

25
00:01:52,671 --> 00:01:56,957
So field weakening buys this motor just a
fifth more speed:

26
00:01:56,997 --> 00:02:00,583
20 A cancels only 15 % of the magnet's
flux.

27
00:02:00,623 --> 00:02:07,509
And that's with R neglected: with it, base
speed drops to about 4,740 rpm.

28
00:02:08,509 --> 00:02:12,177
The interior-magnet variant from Lesson 10
does better.

29
00:02:12,217 --> 00:02:15,137
Its voltage limit is an ellipse,

30
00:02:15,177 --> 00:02:20,439
centred at −λm/L_d = −31 A, close to the
20 A circle.

31
00:02:20,479 --> 00:02:26,932
Its base speed is similar, but its power
stays near 400 W out to 10,000 rpm,

32
00:02:26,972 --> 00:02:30,835
and it keeps turning to about 18,400 rpm.

33
00:02:30,875 --> 00:02:36,411
The price: at −20 A, only 36 % of its
magnet flux remains on the d axis,

34
00:02:36,451 --> 00:02:40,824
so check the magnets for demagnetisation
when hot.

35
00:02:41,824 --> 00:02:44,585
Next, the angle. Can we drop the encoder?

36
00:02:44,625 --> 00:02:46,881
The back-EMF carries the angle:

37
00:02:46,921 --> 00:02:52,392
a vector 90° ahead of the rotor's d axis,
as long as the rotor turns.

38
00:02:52,432 --> 00:02:57,442
It's hidden behind R and L, so we run a
software copy of the motor's R–L circuit,

39
00:02:57,482 --> 00:03:02,829
driven by the voltage we command, and
compare its current with the measured one.

40
00:03:02,869 --> 00:03:07,742
The difference corrects a back-EMF
estimate until the currents agree:

41
00:03:07,782 --> 00:03:11,843
a back-EMF observer. It needs R and L, but
not λm.

42
00:03:12,843 --> 00:03:15,947
To get a clean angle and speed from that
estimate,

43
00:03:15,987 --> 00:03:19,113
ê, we lock onto it with a phase-locked
loop.

44
00:03:19,153 --> 00:03:25,194
Turn ê back by our angle estimate, θ̂. If
θ̂ is right, it points along the q axis.

45
00:03:25,234 --> 00:03:30,711
If not, a d component appears,
proportional to the sine of the error.

46
00:03:30,751 --> 00:03:34,535
PI control drives it to zero: its output
is the speed,

47
00:03:34,575 --> 00:03:39,820
and integrating the speed gives the angle.
Ours is tuned for 50 Hz.

48
00:03:40,820 --> 00:03:45,308
Here's a simulated test: 10 A on q, the
current loop on an encoder,

49
00:03:45,348 --> 00:03:50,169
a dynamometer stepping the speed down, and
the observer alongside.

50
00:03:50,209 --> 00:03:58,357
At 3000 rpm, the two angles agree within
0.1°. At 1000, within 0.4°.

51
00:03:58,397 --> 00:04:05,739
At 300 rpm, the error swings by 7°; at
100, by 18°; at 30, by 32°.

52
00:04:05,779 --> 00:04:10,433
At standstill there's no back-EMF at all:
the estimate sits 27° off,

53
00:04:10,473 --> 00:04:13,873
and nothing in the signals can correct it.

54
00:04:14,873 --> 00:04:18,719
With an ideal inverter, the same observer
stays within two

55
00:04:18,759 --> 00:04:23,607
hundredths of a degree down to 30 rpm. The
trouble is the inverter.

56
00:04:23,647 --> 00:04:27,937
Dead time, from Lesson 17, takes 0.24 V
from each phase:

57
00:04:27,977 --> 00:04:33,749
an error the observer can't see, so it
lands in the back-EMF estimate.

58
00:04:33,789 --> 00:04:38,124
That error doesn't shrink with speed, but
the back-EMF does.

59
00:04:38,164 --> 00:04:41,879
Their fundamentals are equal at about
112 rpm.

60
00:04:41,919 --> 00:04:44,497
So sensorless drives start in open loop,

61
00:04:44,537 --> 00:04:50,337
and hand over to the observer once the
back-EMF stands well clear of such errors.

62
00:04:51,337 --> 00:04:56,548
Last, losses: every watt from the bus
either reaches the shaft or becomes heat.

63
00:04:56,588 --> 00:04:59,659
Copper, 3/2 R |i|²: 24 W at 20 A.

64
00:04:59,699 --> 00:05:05,149
It follows the torque, and any i_d spent
on weakening.

65
00:05:05,189 --> 00:05:07,688
Iron: Lesson 4's model for this steel,

66
00:05:07,728 --> 00:05:11,629
with Lesson 5's flux densities. It follows
the speed.

67
00:05:11,669 --> 00:05:16,189
Then MOSFET conduction and switching, with
assumed devices, and friction.

68
00:05:16,229 --> 00:05:25,165
At 3000 rpm and 0.39 N·m, the shaft gets
122.5 W. Copper takes 6.6,

69
00:05:25,205 --> 00:05:33,878
iron 4.7, friction 1.6, the inverter 1.3.
Efficiency: 89.6 %.

70
00:05:34,878 --> 00:05:38,993
Repeat that across the torque–speed plane,
and you get an efficiency map.

71
00:05:39,033 --> 00:05:47,342
At low speed and high torque, copper
dominates: 60 % at 500 rpm and 0.7 N·m.

72
00:05:47,382 --> 00:05:53,063
At high speed and light load, iron and
friction take over.

73
00:05:53,103 --> 00:05:59,085
The best point, 90.8 %, sits near
4,900 rpm and 0.54 N·m.

74
00:05:59,125 --> 00:06:03,301
Past base speed, current on −d costs
copper loss without torque,

75
00:06:03,341 --> 00:06:06,072
so efficiency falls toward the edge.

76
00:06:06,112 --> 00:06:09,111
Ripple, magnet and punching losses aren't
modelled:

77
00:06:09,151 --> 00:06:11,618
treat this map as an upper bound.

78
00:06:12,618 --> 00:06:16,121
Two experiments to close. First, map the
efficiency:

79
00:06:16,161 --> 00:06:21,973
a power analyser on the DC bus, a torque
sensor and a load machine on the shaft.

80
00:06:22,013 --> 00:06:27,420
Efficiency is shaft power over DC power.
At 0.39 N·m,

81
00:06:27,460 --> 00:06:33,267
a torque error of just 0.005 N·m moves it
by a point.

82
00:06:33,307 --> 00:06:38,626
Second, log observer and encoder angles
while stepping the speed down:

83
00:06:38,666 --> 00:06:44,601
under half a degree at 1000 rpm, 20 or 30
degrees at 100 rpm and below.

84
00:06:45,606 --> 00:06:48,272
That's the end of the series. Looking
back:

85
00:06:48,312 --> 00:06:52,547
We began with a compass needle beside a
wire, put windings in slots,

86
00:06:52,588 --> 00:06:57,726
let iron and magnets shape the field, and
found the back-EMF and the torque.

87
00:06:57,766 --> 00:07:01,937
Clarke and Park turned three alternating
currents into two steady ones,

88
00:07:01,977 --> 00:07:04,531
and the dq model gave their voltages.

89
00:07:04,571 --> 00:07:07,640
The inverter makes those voltages. Current
control,

90
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:12,430
field-oriented control, field weakening
and the observer close the loop.

91
00:07:12,470 --> 00:07:17,058
At every step: a model, its limits, and a
number you can check on a bench.

92
00:07:18,058 --> 00:07:19,874
Three capstone questions.

93
00:07:19,914 --> 00:07:25,326
One: our surface-magnet motor spins
unloaded at 5,800 rpm. Neglecting R

94
00:07:25,366 --> 00:07:31,626
and the losses, what i_d does it need, and
what copper loss does that cost?

95
00:07:32,226 --> 00:07:36,452
Two: at standstill, why can high-frequency
injection find the angle

96
00:07:36,492 --> 00:07:41,067
of the interior-magnet rotor, but not of
the surface-magnet one?

97
00:07:41,667 --> 00:07:45,033
Three: rewind with twice the turns in the
same slots,

98
00:07:45,073 --> 00:07:48,633
so λm doubles and R and L quadruple. At
the same torque,

99
00:07:48,673 --> 00:07:53,286
what happens to the current, the copper
loss and the base speed?

100
00:07:53,886 --> 00:07:56,417
Worked answers are in the lesson's
repository.

101
00:07:56,457 --> 00:07:58,746
Thanks for following the series.
